## Micrometer to CloudWatch

```xml
<!-- pom.xml -->
<dependency>
    <groupId>io.micrometer</groupId>
    <artifactId>micrometer-registry-cloudwatch2</artifactId>
</dependency>
```

```yaml
# application.yml
management:
  metrics:
    export:
      cloudwatch:
        namespace: MyApp/Production
        step: 1m          # publish every minute
        batch-size: 20
  endpoints:
    web:
      exposure:
        include: health, info, metrics, prometheus
```

```java
// Custom business metrics
@Service
public class OrderService {
    private final MeterRegistry meterRegistry;
    private final Counter orderCounter;
    private final Timer orderProcessingTimer;

    public OrderService(MeterRegistry registry) {
        this.meterRegistry = registry;
        this.orderCounter = Counter.builder("orders.created")
            .description("Total orders created")
            .tag("status", "success")
            .register(registry);
        this.orderProcessingTimer = Timer.builder("orders.processing.time")
            .register(registry);
    }

    public Order createOrder(OrderRequest req) {
        return orderProcessingTimer.record(() -> {
            Order order = doCreateOrder(req);
            orderCounter.increment();
            return order;
        });
    }
}
```

## CloudWatch Alarms via AWS CLI

```bash
# Alarm: 5xx error rate > 1% for 5 minutes
aws cloudwatch put-metric-alarm \
  --alarm-name high-5xx-rate \
  --metric-name HTTPCode_Target_5XX_Count \
  --namespace AWS/ApplicationELB \
  --dimensions Name=LoadBalancer,Value=app/my-alb/xxx \
  --period 300 \
  --evaluation-periods 2 \
  --statistic Sum \
  --threshold 10 \
  --comparison-operator GreaterThanThreshold \
  --alarm-actions arn:aws:sns:...:notify-team
```

## Structured Logging for CloudWatch

```java
// Use JSON logging — easier to filter in CloudWatch Logs Insights
// logback-spring.xml or via dependency: logstash-logback-encoder
@RestControllerAdvice
public class GlobalExceptionHandler {
    private static final Logger log = LoggerFactory.getLogger(GlobalExceptionHandler.class);

    @ExceptionHandler(Exception.class)
    public ResponseEntity<ErrorResponse> handle(Exception ex, HttpServletRequest req) {
        log.error("Unhandled error",
            kv("path", req.getRequestURI()),
            kv("method", req.getMethod()),
            kv("errorType", ex.getClass().getSimpleName()),
            ex);
        return ResponseEntity.status(500).body(new ErrorResponse(ex.getMessage()));
    }
}
```

```
# CloudWatch Logs Insights query — find top errors in last hour
fields @timestamp, errorType, path
| filter level = 'ERROR'
| stats count(*) as errorCount by errorType, path
| sort errorCount desc
| limit 20
```

## Spring Cloud AWS Integration

```xml
<dependency>
    <groupId>io.awspring.cloud</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-cloud-aws-starter-secrets-manager</artifactId>
</dependency>
```

```yaml
# application.yml — reference secrets by name, not by value
spring:
  config:
    import: "aws-secretsmanager:/prod/myapp/database,/prod/myapp/redis"

  datasource:
    url: jdbc:postgresql://${host}:5432/${dbname}
    username: ${username}
    password: ${password}
    # Spring Cloud AWS resolves these from the secret JSON:
    # {
    #   "host": "mydb.xyz.rds.amazonaws.com",
    #   "dbname": "appdb",
    #   "username": "appuser",
    #   "password": "rotated-password-here"
    # }
```

## Direct SDK Access (No Spring Cloud AWS)

```java
@Configuration
public class SecretsConfig {

    @Bean
    public DatabaseCredentials databaseCredentials() {
        SecretsManagerClient client = SecretsManagerClient.builder()
            .region(Region.AP_SOUTH_1)
            .build();

        GetSecretValueResponse response = client.getSecretValue(
            GetSecretValueRequest.builder()
                .secretId("/prod/myapp/database")
                .build()
        );

        // Parse JSON secret
        return objectMapper.readValue(response.secretString(), DatabaseCredentials.class);
    }
}
```

